 Why: a plain `run:` value that contains a colon followed by a space (for example `--only-binary=:all: -r`) is NOT
 valid YAML. GitHub then shows the run as failed with no jobs, and a check can stay "queued" for ever. No other test
 parsed the files. PyYAML is in the development lock (requirements-dev.txt), so the check needs no new dependency.
+
+What is checked (structure only, in `*.yml` and `*.yaml` files): valid YAML; a top-level `on` (PyYAML 6 reads the
+bare word `on` as the boolean True, so both spellings are accepted); jobs that each have `runs-on` (or `uses`, for a
+reusable workflow call, which needs no steps) and only the job keys of the GitHub workflow syntax; steps that each have
+exactly one of `run` or `uses` and only the step keys of that syntax. A misspelled key is therefore found.
+
+What is NOT checked, and cannot be with a YAML parser: the text of an `if:` expression (for example `=` instead of
+`==`) and the names inside `${{ }}` (for example a typo such as `runnr.os`). `actionlint` is the real control for those:
+measured, actionlint 1.7.12 reports both and this test does not. It is NOT yet wired into CI (no workflow runs it), so
+until someone adds it as a CI step those mistakes are found only when GitHub runs the workflow.
 """
 
